      "knowledge_point": "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators",
      "problem": "$\\frac{6}{2x-1}-\\frac{3}{x}=$",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $x(2x-1),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $x,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2x-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{2x-1}-\\frac{3}{x} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{x}\\cdot \\frac{6}{2x-1}-\\frac{2x-1}{2x-1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} & = \\\\ \\frac{6x}{x(2x-1)}-\\frac{3(2x-1)}{x(2x-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6x-3(2x-1)}{x(2x-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6x-6x+3}{x(2x-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{x(2x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
