      "knowledge_point": "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression",
      "problem": "Write $5+\\frac{h-3}{h}$ as a single fraction.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $5=\\frac{5}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $h$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{h}{h}\\cdot \\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5h}{h}+\\frac{h-3}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5h}{h}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5h+h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{6h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(2h-1)}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
